Answer: This sentence is in the past simple tense, and the verb in the underline is in the past simple tense too.

\begin{blockquote}
He has been reading the book for the past two hours.
\end{blockquote}

A past perfect tense would be needed in this case if it were:

\begin{blockquote}
He had been reading the book for the past two hours.
\end{blockquote}

To identify if the tense is in the past simple or the past perfect:

\begin{blockquote}
\begin{itemize}
\item The past simple tense is used for an action that happened at a particular point in time in the past.
\item The past perfect tense is used for an action that happened in the past but finished before another action in the past.
\end{itemize}
\end{blockquote}

In this case, the action (reading the book) finished at the particular point of time (two hours ago). So the sentence is in the past simple tense.

Answer: The sentence is in the past simple tense, as the action is finished, and the verb has to be in the past simple tense.

\begin{blockquote}
\begin{itemize}
\item The present simple tense is used for something that happens regularly.
\item The present perfect tense is used for something that happened recently.
\item The past simple tense is used for an action that happened at a particular point in time in the past.
\item The past perfect tense is used for an action that happened in the past but finished before another action in the past.
\end{itemize}
\end{blockquote}

