Answer: I would use `std::remove_if`, like so:

\begin{code}
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <algorithm>

int main()
{
    std::string s = "My favorite food is very yummy.";
    std::remove_if(s.begin(), s.end(), [](char c) { return c == 'v' || c == 'V'; });
    std::cout << s << std::endl;
}
\end{code}

If you're not familiar with `std::remove_if`, it's a member function of `std::string` that accepts a predicate to remove elements, as shown in the example above. It starts with the first element of the string and the predicate, and iterates over the rest of the string. If the predicate is true, the element is removed from the string and the next element is examined with the predicate.

Note that `std::remove_if` is more efficient than your solution because it operates in-place.

